#1180 is fixed and committed as a86656ca. A line like directories = { deny = ["/etc"] } no longer gets the "directories is not a key yoyo reads" message. yoyo now refuses it and says plainly that the rule is not in force. Build, clippy (-D warnings), fmt and the full cargo test all pass.

What I chose: I went with refusing rather than reading, as the task suggested. Each warning also gives the user their own line in the form yoyo does read. From yoyo config show on the built binary, with a temporary HOME and working directory:
```
warning: .yoyo.toml line 1: `directories = { ... }` is valid TOML, but yoyo does not read that
spelling of `directories`, so it has no effect. This rule is NOT in force. yoyo reads
`[directories]` then `allow = [...]` / `deny = [...]`. Write it as:
    [directories]
    deny = ["/etc"]
```
permissions gets the same warning. mcp_servers = { fs = { command = "npx" } } is told to write [mcp_servers.fs] / command = "npx", without the "NOT in force" sentence, because it isn't a fence.

Evidence:
• Coverage: yoyo reads four table names (permissions, directories, mcp_servers, model_pricing), and each has test rows. I added 12 rows to the existing comparison against Python's tomllib. Before the fix, 10 of the 12 disagreed with it; after, none do.
• The suggested fix is tested by using it: in 11 of the 12 rows, the test swaps the user's line for the printed suggestion and reads it with yoyo's own parsers. The result must match what tomllib reads from the original line, with no warning left.
• The test can fail: I deliberately broke the suggestion (emptied the values), and that test failed by name. I put the code back and confirmed the marker was gone.
• Exact wording: a separate test pins the full directories warning and the nested "my server" case.

Limits:
• An inline table whose array spans several lines is still refused, but it only gets the general hint, not a copy of the user's line.
• The comment on the test rows no longer claims that the values are covered by the other test file (that file only uses the key k). It now says what these rows vary and what they keep fixed.
• One case is still not covered: an inline table under a header, such as [mcp_servers] then fs = { command = "npx" }. yoyo already says it doesn't read this (the user isn't blamed for a typo), but it isn't read and gets no suggested rewrite. I filed it as #1182 before committing.

I also added a sentence to docs/src/configuration/permissions.md saying inline tables aren't read and what the warning gives you. src/config.rs wasn't touched, so no module-size update was needed.
